WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
Film Courage: Why is it important for writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:06.508
to understand that the idea of the&nbsp;
story is not the whole story well?

00:00:06.508 --> 00:00:07.720
Alan Watt, Author/Screenwriter and [Founder&nbsp;
of L.A. Writers’ Lab]: I do always say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:11.800
to my writers is that your idea of your&nbsp;
story is never the whole story. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:21.240
that the idea is incorrect, it's the the idea&nbsp;
is incomplete all right and so being a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:26.680
being a storyteller, being an artist is humbling&nbsp;
because you're not the author you're the channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:34.400
When I'm working with writers I'm really&nbsp;
speaking to their higher self. I'm talking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:41.000
I really believe that your subconscious is the&nbsp;
seed of your genius and the genius is not a noun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:50.000
it's a verb it's something we all have access to&nbsp;
and so you have to let go of your idea of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.040
story you have to let go of your ego because&nbsp;
as Einstein says you can't solve a problem at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.760
the same level of consciousness that created the&nbsp;
problem and every story begins with an apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.040
dramatic problem all right but you can't solve it&nbsp;
at the same level of consciousness that created it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:15.320
so what you've got to do is you've got to you've&nbsp;
got to let go of this idea of what the story is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:23.440
in order for the truth of the story to emerge&nbsp;
if I was to reduce or distill story structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.840
to three words I'd say it's desire protagonist&nbsp;
has to want something that stakes your life and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.360
death if I don't get blank my life will be&nbsp;
unimaginable if Jan Brady doesn't get a date&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.640
with Tad Hamilton she will positively die all&nbsp;
right and then surrender your protagonist has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.040
to let go of the meaning they made out of their&nbsp;
goal all right and then finally transformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:54.800
so desire surrender transformation I'm really&nbsp;
a big believer in the three act structure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:02.040
so the first act is all about thesis it's the&nbsp;
argument it's the dramatic question stated act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:08.920
two is about antithesis it's the drama played out&nbsp;
and then act three is synthesis or integration how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:16.920
can we take these two seemingly disparate ideas&nbsp;
and that collide and lead and lead us to a deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:29.480
truth so and the two these two seemingly disparate&nbsp;
ideas involve the dilemma so for example justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:35.840
would be powerful desire I want Justice the false&nbsp;
belief is that when I kill my enemy justice will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.640
be done by the end of the story your protagonist&nbsp;
reframes their relationship to what justice means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.760
they might discover that true justice means&nbsp;
taking responsibility, discovering perhaps that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:52.600
I'm complicit in this thing. It might be a mystery&nbsp;
story or a thriller where they discover that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.600 --> 00:03:01.760
their attempt to uncover justice they discover&nbsp;
something about their beloved that puts get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:09.866
creates a dilemma where if I do kill my enemy&nbsp;
I will in some way be complicit in the crime.

00:03:09.866 --> 00:03:15.040
Film Courage: Now you say that we are not the&nbsp;
author, we are the channel. If we take something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.760
let's say like Oppenheimer (an amazing&nbsp;
film) that's based on someone's life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.480
How are we actually a channel when&nbsp;
that life has already occurred?

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.880
Al: Ah it's a great question, so if you gave the&nbsp;
Oppenheimer story to 10 writers they're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.040
write 10 completely different stories right.&nbsp;
Not completely different I mean they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:40.520
have to they would have to be some way germane&nbsp;
to the the events of his life but the story is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:47.080
not about what happens it's about the meaning we&nbsp;
ascribe to what happens I'll give you an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.880
so let's let's just put Oppenheimer for aside for&nbsp;
a second somebody asked me years ago one of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:56.040
students they said you're always talking about&nbsp;
meaning what are you talking about I said well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.840
I'm going to tell you a story right now I just&nbsp;
made something up and now this is the stupid good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.840
example I always give I'm going to make up a story&nbsp;
I'm going to tell you exactly what happened but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:11.360
will be bereft of meaning so woman comes home says&nbsp;
to her husband we've been married 20 years I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:19.400
a divorce husband quits his job gets on a plane&nbsp;
flies to France goes out to a carrot Farm the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:26.240
farmer rushes out hugs the man and together they&nbsp;
open a carrot juice restaurant called carrot juice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:32.280
Charlie I just told you exactly what happened&nbsp;
doesn't mean anything so now here's the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:37.320
with meaning woman comes home says to her husband&nbsp;
we've been married 20 years and you're still a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:43.240
stranger to me the man quits his job and flies to&nbsp;
France which is the last place he saw his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.240
alive when he was 8 years old he goes out to the&nbsp;
carrot Farm where his father dropped dead of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:55.800
heart attack the farmer who was the father's&nbsp;
best friend rushes out and hugs the son still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:04.120
distraught over his inability to save the man&nbsp;
leaving the boy orphaned together they open a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:11.560
carrot juice restaurant in honor of the the boy's&nbsp;
father whose name was Charlie and they begin the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:19.000
process of healing so it's not what happens it's&nbsp;
the meaning we ascribe to it I can't tell you how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:28.000
many writers I work with that will say they'll&nbsp;
say well my character would never do that and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:36.040
answer is always well if you have an Impulse if&nbsp;
an idea comes to you and you dismiss it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:43.000
you think your idea is your character wouldn't do&nbsp;
that you're actually cutting off the channel your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:51.000
job as a storyteller I believe is to support what&nbsp;
your subconscious gives you I can't tell you how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:58.000
many times I see second acts start to flatline&nbsp;
because we get so attached to our idea of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:05.120
the story ought to go that we disconnect from the&nbsp;
truth of what the story wants to be I'll give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.960
another example I don't know why I always bring&nbsp;
up It's a Wonderful Life but I don't know if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:17.880
remember the midpoint of It's a Wonderful Life but&nbsp;
this is where Mr Potter offers George Bailey a job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:27.560
now imagine for a moment that you're writing this&nbsp;
story that would make no sense logically for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:34.080
devil for the the the the primary antagonist&nbsp;
to offer the hero a job because they're in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:39.480
direct competition with each other but here's the&nbsp;
thing as a storyteller he doesn't offer the George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.960
Bailey from act one a job he offers the George&nbsp;
Bailey from the middle of act two a job this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.360
is the midpoint this is where your protagonist&nbsp;
experiences temptation between what they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:55.760
and what they need and George barley considers&nbsp;
taking the job because this is how we experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:01.960
how desperately he wants to leave Bedford Falls&nbsp;
that he would actually con consider working for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:11.960
the devil so this is why story structure is so&nbsp;
important and this is why an outline particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:22.120
in screenwriting is essential because without an&nbsp;
outline your idea of your story is never going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:27.720
to get you to the end because you're going to be&nbsp;
so attached to what you think should happen that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:36.760
you're not going to allow yourself to truly be&nbsp;
surprised by where the characters want to take you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:41.880
if you're not allowing yourself to be a little bit&nbsp;
embarrassed a little bit vulnerable you're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:47.280
to be protecting yourself in some way the first&nbsp;
exercise I give my writers in the workshops is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:52.360
fear I call it the fear exercise and the exercise&nbsp;
is simply I'm afraid to write this story because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:57.280
you write for five minutes listing all your fears&nbsp;
and I and then I asked the writer I go why do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.680
think we do this exercise and they always go well&nbsp;
it's probably really helpful to become aware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:07.720
your fears so they don't rule you and I go well&nbsp;
that's part of it for sure but here's the deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:15.800
